  "activity": {
    "id": "vectors_projectile_motion_lab",
    "name": "Vectors and Projectile Motion AR Lab",
    "description": "A comprehensive AR training experience for General Physics I students exploring vectors as mathematical tools for representing magnitude and direction, and their application to two-dimensional projectile motion. Students work through abstract vector concepts (magnitude, direction, addition, decomposition) before applying these tools to understand how projectiles move under gravity with independent horizontal and vertical components. The lab culminates in range prediction, targeting challenges, and free-form exploration.",
    "language": "en",
    "workplace": "physics_vectors_projectile",
    "start": "intro_vectors_foundation",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "intro_vectors_foundation",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_user",
        "location": "workspace_vectors",
        "predicate": "intro_vector_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Introduction to Vectors: Magnitude and Direction",
          "description": "A single vector arrow appears floating in the 3D workspace with its tail at the origin and head extending outward. The arrow shows both magnitude (arrow length) and direction (angle relative to x, y, z axes which are displayed as colored reference lines). Narration introduces vectors as quantities with both size (magnitude) and direction in space, contrasting them with scalar quantities like temperature that have only size. You can grab the arrow's head and drag it around in 3D space; observe how the magnitude and direction labels update in real time. Try rotating your view around the arrow to confirm it does not change\u2014only your perspective does."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "coordinate_axes_3d"
            },
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vector_magnitude_label",
              "poi": "arrow_head"
            },
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vector_direction_label",
              "poi": "arrow_head"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "hololens_user",
              "type": "device",
              "text": "Vector lab initialized. Use hand gestures to manipulate the arrow.",
              "viewport": "actions"
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "intro_vectors_foundation",
            "type": "action",
            "value": "next",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "vector_addition_scene",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_user",
        "location": "workspace_vectors",
        "predicate": "vector_addition_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Vector Addition by the Tip-to-Tail Method",
          "description": "Two vectors now appear in the workspace in different colors\u2014blue for the first vector and green for the second. The blue vector starts at the origin; the green vector is positioned tip-to-tail, with its tail at the head of the blue vector. A third arrow (the resultant, in red) is drawn from the origin to the head of the green vector. Narration explains that vector addition produces the resultant: when two vectors are placed tip-to-tail, the sum points from the beginning of the first to the end of the second. You can grab either the blue or green vector and reorient it; the resultant updates continuously. Try swapping the order\u2014move the green vector to start at the origin and place the blue vector tip-to-tail on its head. Observe that the resultant points to the same final location: vector addition is commutative."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "vector_pair_addition",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vector_addition_narration"
            },
            {
              "target": "vector_pair_addition",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "resultant_vector_visual"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "hololens_user",
              "type": "device",
              "text": "Grab the vector heads to reorient them. The resultant updates in real time."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "vector_pair_addition",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "vector_addition_scene",
            "type": "action",
            "value": "next",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "vector_decomposition_scene",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_user",
        "location": "workspace_vectors",
        "predicate": "decomposition_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Vector Decomposition: Breaking Into Components",
          "description": "A single vector appears in neutral color. Three smaller component arrows emerge from the origin, one along each axis (x-red, y-green, z-blue), arranged tip-to-tail: the x component starts at the origin, the y component begins at the x tip, and the z component begins at the y tip, ending at the head of the original vector. Narration introduces decomposition as the reverse of addition\u2014breaking the original vector into pieces along chosen axes. The three components add tip-to-tail to reproduce the original vector. You can grab and resize each component individually along its axis; the original vector updates to reflect the new sum. Note that the magnitudes of the components relate to the original vector through the three-dimensional Pythagorean theorem: the magnitude of the original vector squared equals the sum of the squares of the three component magnitudes."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "component_arrows_xyz"
            },
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "pythagorean_theorem_label",
              "poi": "arrow_head"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "hololens_user",
              "type": "device",
              "text": "Grab each component arrow and slide it along its axis to see the original vector update."
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "arrow_single_vector",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "vector_decomposition_scene",
            "type": "action",
            "value": "next",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "transition_to_projectile",
        "viewport": "actions",
        "type": "actions",
        "device": "hololens_user",
        "location": "workspace_projectile",
        "predicate": "projectile_transition_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "From Abstract Vectors to Projectile Motion",
          "description": "The abstract vector workspace gives way to a 2D vertical plane rendered in the AR environment with horizontal (x) and vertical (y) axes clearly labeled. Narration explains that the vector tools just learned will now be applied to a specific physical problem: the motion of a projectile launched into the air. Because gravity acts only downward (a force with no horizontal component) and there are no other horizontal forces, the entire motion takes place in a vertical plane containing the initial velocity vector. Although the world is three-dimensional, projectile motion is genuinely two-dimensional. A small ball rests on the ground line at the left side of the plane with a velocity vector arrow drawn from it pointing upward and to the right at an initial launch angle."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "workspace_projectile",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "projectile_2d_plane"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "initial_velocity_decomposition"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
              "target": "hololens_user",
              "type": "device",
              "text": "The vector decomposition you learned is now applied to projectile motion. Ready to launch?"
            }
          ],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "workspace_projectile",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ],
          "messages": [],
          "if_logic": []
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "voice",
            "id": "transition_to_projectile",
            "type": "action",
            "value": "launch",
            "operator": "equal"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "projectile_flight_demonstration",
        "viewport": "reactions",
        "type": "reactions",
        "device": "hololens_user",
        "location": "workspace_projectile",
        "predicate": "projectile_flight_narration",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Projectile Motion: Independent Horizontal and Vertical Components",
          "description": "The ball is launched. As it flies, two component vectors are continuously drawn on the ball: a horizontal arrow (pointing right, constant length throughout flight) and a vertical arrow (pointing upward initially, shrinking during ascent, becoming zero at the peak, then pointing downward and growing during descent). The ball traces a parabolic path shown as a faint trail behind it. Narration emphasizes the central insight: the horizontal and vertical motions are independent. The horizontal motion is unaffected by gravity\u2014the ball moves rightward at constant velocity, exactly as it would without gravity. The vertical motion is exactly what a ball thrown straight up would do\u2014it slows, stops, and falls. The parabolic trajectory emerges from these two independent motions occurring simultaneously. You can switch between side view (see the parabola), overhead view (motion appears as a straight line\u2014gravity has no effect on horizontal motion), and other angles."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "trajectory_animation"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "component_vectors_live"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "horizontal_velocity_label",
              "poi": "ball_center"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "vertical_velocity_label",
              "poi": "ball_center"
            },
            {
              "target": "projectile_ball",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "maximum_height_indicator"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": [],
          "messages": [
            {
